☕ Java
Java is a high-level, object-oriented programming language.
It is platform-independent because programs run on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).
Famous for its slogan: “Write once, run anywhere.”

⚙️ JVM, JDK, JRE
JVM (Java Virtual Machine): Runs Java programs by converting bytecode into machine code. It makes Java platform-independent.
JRE (Java Runtime Environment): Provides libraries + JVM needed to run Java applications.
JDK (Java Development Kit): Full package for developers. Includes JRE + tools (compiler, debugger) to write and run Java programs.

📦 Variables
A variable is a named storage location in memory that holds data.
Example: int age = 20; → age is the variable storing the value 20.

📝 Naming Rules
Must start with a letter, underscore (_), or dollar sign ($).
Cannot start with a number.
No spaces or special characters.
Case-sensitive (Age ≠ age).
Should be meaningful (e.g., studentName instead of x).

🔢 Primitive Data Types
These are the basic building blocks in Java:
byte (small integers)
short (medium integers)
int (normal integers)
long (large integers)
float (decimal numbers, less precise)
double (decimal numbers, more precise)
char (single character, e.g., 'A')
boolean (true/false)

📚 Non-Primitive Data Types
Also called reference types.
Examples: Strings, Arrays, Classes, Interfaces.
They are created by the programmer or provided by Java libraries.
Unlike primitives, they can be null.

🔄 Type Casting (Introduction)
Type casting means converting one data type into another.
Two types:
Implicit (Widening): Smaller type → larger type (safe). Example: int → double.
Explicit (Narrowing): Larger type → smaller type (may lose data). Example: double → int.




