📌 What is an Array?
An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in contiguous memory locations.
Example: int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

📌 Why Arrays?
To store multiple values under a single name instead of creating many variables.
Makes data management easier (e.g., storing marks of 100 students).
Supports fast access using index numbers.

📌 Indexing
Each element in an array has a unique index.
Index starts from 0.
Example: arr[0] = 10, arr[1] = 20.

📌 Memory Representation
Elements are stored sequentially in memory.
If the base address is 1000 and each integer takes 4 bytes:
arr[0] → 1000
arr[1] → 1004
arr[2] → 1008 … and so on.

📌 Traversing an Array
Traversing means visiting each element one by one.
Example:
java
for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
📌 Time Complexity
Access (arr[i]) → O(1): Direct access using index.
Search → O(n): Need to check each element one by one in worst case.

