Value:  10   20   30   40   50



🖥️ Coding with Arrays
1️⃣ Print an Array
java
public class PrintArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(arr[i]);
        }
    }
}

2️⃣ Find Sum of Elements
java
public class SumArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int sum = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
    }
}

3️⃣ Find Maximum
java
public class MaxArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int max = arr[0];
        for(int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if(arr[i] > max) {
                max = arr[i];
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Maximum = " + max);
    }
}

4️⃣ Find Minimum
java
public class MinArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int min = arr[0];
        for(int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if(arr[i] < min) {
                min = arr[i];
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Minimum = " + min);
    }
}

5️⃣ Find Average
java
public class AverageArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int sum = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        double average = (double) sum / arr.length;
        System.out.println("Average = " + average);
    }
}


🎤 Interview Questions
❓ Why does array indexing start at 0?
Because the index is an offset from the base address in memory.
arr[0] means “0 steps away from the base address.”
This makes memory calculation simple and efficient.

✅ Advantages of Arrays
Fast access using index (O(1)).
Easy to manage multiple values under one name.
Contiguous memory improves cache performance.

⚠️ Disadvantages of Arrays
Fixed size (cannot grow/shrink dynamically).
Insertion and deletion are costly (need shifting).
Wasted memory if size is larger than needed.

⏱️ Time Complexity of Searching
Linear Search (unsorted array): O(n)
Binary Search (sorted array): O(log n)



🔢 Why does array indexing start at 0?
Historical reason: In low‑level languages like C, the array name represents the address of the first element.
Formula simplicity: The address of element i is calculated as

Base Address+𝑖⋅
Size of each element
If indexing started at 1, the formula would need an extra subtraction (i-1), making it less efficient.
Consistency: Zero‑based indexing aligns naturally with pointer arithmetic and memory offsets.

✅ Advantages of arrays
Fast access: Direct access to elements using index (constant time, 𝑂(1)).
Memory efficiency: Stores elements in contiguous memory locations.
Ease of traversal: Simple iteration using loops.
Better cache performance: Contiguous storage improves CPU caching.
Useful for fixed‑size collections: Ideal when the number of elements is known in advance.

❌ Disadvantages of arrays
Fixed size: Cannot grow or shrink dynamically (unless using dynamic arrays like ArrayList in Java).
Insertion/Deletion cost: Shifting elements makes these operations expensive (𝑂(𝑛)).
Wasted memory: If allocated size is larger than needed.
Homogeneous data only: Can store only one data type.
No built‑in flexibility: Unlike linked lists, arrays don’t adapt well to frequent structural changes.

📚 Database
A database is an organized collection of data.
Think of it like a digital filing cabinet where information is stored and managed.

🗂️ Table
A table is a structure inside a database that stores data in rows and columns.
Example: A Students table with columns like ID, Name, Age.

📄 Row
A row (also called a record/tuple) represents one entry in a table.
Example: ID=1, Name='Sanjay', Age=20.

📊 Column
A column defines a field/attribute of the table.
Example: Name column stores all student names.

🔑 Primary Key
A primary key uniquely identifies each row in a table.
Rules: Must be unique and not null.
Example: StudentID in a Students table.

🔍 SELECT Statement
Used to retrieve data from a table.
Syntax:

sql
SELECT column1, column2 
FROM table_name 
WHERE condition;
Example:

sql
SELECT Name, Age 
FROM Students 
WHERE Age > 18

| id | name  | age |
| -- | ----- | --- |
| 1  | Rahul | 20  |
| 2  | Priya | 21  |
| 3  | Arun  | 19  |

📌 Show all data
sql
SELECT * 
FROM Students;
(Retrieves every column and every row from the table.)

📌 Show only names
sql
SELECT Name 
FROM Students;
(Displays just the Name column.)

📌 Show age column
sql
SELECT Age 
FROM Students;
(Shows only the Age values.)

📌 Show id and name
sql
SELECT ID, Name 
FROM Students;
(Retrieves two specific columns: ID and Name.)

📌 Show all columns
sql
SELECT * 
FROM Students;

🗄️ Difference between DBMS and RDBMS
DBMS (Database Management System)
Manages data in a database.
Stores data as files or tables, but relationships between data are not enforced.
Example: MS Access, File System DB.

RDBMS (Relational Database Management System)
Stores data in tables with relationships (rows & columns).
Enforces constraints like Primary Key, Foreign Key.
Supports SQL for querying.
Example: MySQL, Oracle, SQL Server.

👉 Key point: RDBMS is a type of DBMS with relational features.

🔑 What is a Primary Key?
A Primary Key is a column (or set of columns) that uniquely identifies each row in a table.
Rules:
Must be unique.
Cannot be NULL.
Example: In a Students table, StudentID can be the primary key.

💻 What is SQL?
SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language used to interact with databases.
Functions:
DQL: Query data (SELECT).

DML: Modify data (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).

DDL: Define structure (CREATE, ALTER, DROP).

